{"chunk_id": "88fa1bba450f", "text": "Duryodhana, consumed by jealousy after witnessing Yudhishthira\u2019s immense prosperity and the grandeur of the Rajasuya sacrifice, confesses despair to his uncle Sakuni and even contemplates suicide. He believes fate favors the Pandavas and that his own efforts have failed while their wealth, alliances, and fame continue to grow. Sakuni first reminds him of the Pandavas\u2019 powerful allies and victories, then reveals a cunning alternative: they cannot be defeated in battle, but Yudhishthira has a weakness\u2014dice. Skilled in deceitful gambling, Sakuni proposes inviting Yudhishthira to a game he cannot refuse and stripping him of his kingdom. Duryodhana eagerly supports the plan and pressures Dhritarashtra to permit it, threatening self-destruction if refused. This moment is pivotal because it sets in motion the infamous dice game that will lead to the humiliation of the Pandavas and the escalation toward the Kurukshetra war.\nDuryodhana\u2019s jealousy of the Pandavas leads Sakuni to propose defeating Yudhishthira through a deceitful dice game, which they present to Dhritarashtra.\nThemes: jealousy, deception, gambling, political manipulation, envy of prosperity", "vector": [-0.008534361608326435, -0.015869736671447754, 0.006904278416186571, -0.0013926434330642223, -0.02955704741179943, 0.01873922161757946, -0.03308665007352829, 0.0008958722464740276, -0.02788654901087284, -0.034056615084409714, 0.025825368240475655, 0.004546720068901777, 0.034352995455265045, 0.02015375718474388, -0.013195591978728771, 0.017957860603928566, 0.05046522244811058, -0.0022935676388442516, 0.05429120361804962, -0.0323052853345871, -0.012232360430061817, -0.030338410288095474, 0.0020914911292493343, 0.007241072598844767, -0.019628359004855156, 0.028721798211336136, 0.007065939716994762, -0.023360036313533783, 0.004715117160230875, -0.006850391160696745, -0.003785565262660384, 0.00021765317069366574, 0.023467810824513435, -0.014549504034221172, 